<ContentLayout design="v2" structuredData={__sd} title="Office chair sinks: gas cylinder or control mechanism? | StackGeist" description="Diagnose a sinking office chair before ordering parts. Separate the control mechanism from the pneumatic cylinder, identify the exact chair, and follow the manufacturer procedure." active="guides">
<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / Office chair sinking</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Chair troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Office chair sinks: diagnose the control mechanism or gas cylinder first.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">A chair that will not hold height may have a worn pneumatic cylinder, but the height-control mechanism can also be responsible. Generic “universal cylinder” advice skips the part that matters most: the exact chair model and the manufacturer’s service procedure.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>Before ordering parts</strong><p>Find the model and serial label under the chair. Check the manufacturer’s support page for sinking/height-control symptoms, replacement parts, and warranty. If the maker identifies the cylinder as the fault, order the specified compatible part and follow that chair’s removal procedure. Do not drill, cut, puncture, heat, or disassemble the pressurized cylinder.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Diagnosis</p><h2>Sinking does not prove which component failed.</h2>
<p>HON’s current support guidance states that a chair that sinks or will not raise/lower can be caused by either the control mechanism or the pneumatic cylinder. That is a useful general rule: diagnose the actuation/control side before assuming the sealed gas spring itself is the only candidate.</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>Check whether the height lever moves freely and actually engages the mechanism.</li>
<li>Look for visible damage or looseness where the control mechanism meets the cylinder.</li>
<li>Record the model and serial number before ordering anything.</li>
</ul></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Part selection</p><h2>Do not select a replacement from a generic weight-class chart.</h2>
<p>Marketplace listings often advertise “Class 3” and “Class 4” cylinders with broad weight ranges. Those labels do not replace the chair manufacturer’s own occupant rating, cylinder dimensions, control geometry, or approved replacement part. BIFMA chair standards test the complete chair; they are not a universal lookup table that turns a gas-spring class into a safe occupant weight for every chair.</p>
<p>Use the exact chair model, serial number, seat-height range, base/control fit, and manufacturer part number where available.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Replacement</p><h2>Use the procedure for the chair you actually own.</h2>
<p>Removal methods vary. Steelcase, for example, publishes a Gesture cylinder procedure that uses a specific pneumatic-cylinder removal tool and hammer. Other manufacturers may direct owners to a dealer or qualified service person. Follow that model-specific procedure rather than assuming a pipe wrench and mallet are appropriate for every chair.</p>
<div class="note note-warn"><strong>Pressurized component:</strong> do not puncture, drill, cut, grind, heat, burn, or attempt to open a gas cylinder. If the manufacturer does not provide a consumer replacement procedure, use its service channel.</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need a cylinder?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If the manufacturer diagnosis points to the control mechanism, replace/service that part instead.</p></div>
<div><strong>Yes</strong><p>If the exact chair documentation identifies the cylinder and a supported replacement is available, use that part and procedure.</p></div>
<div><strong>Service</strong><p>If the chair is under warranty, uses an undocumented/proprietary mechanism, or the cylinder is seized/damaged, contact the manufacturer or dealer before escalating force.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">The likely cause</p><h2>Do not buy a gas cylinder until you check the height control.</h2>
<p>A chair that sinks might have a bad gas cylinder. It might also have a height-control mechanism that's holding the valve open. Those need different repairs. Before ordering a 'universal' cylinder, get the chair's exact model number and find out which part the manufacturer says fits.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Chair lowers without touching the lever</strong><p>Stop using it in situations where sudden height changes could cause injury. Record the model and use the manufacturer's fault diagnosis to distinguish the cylinder from a control mechanism holding the actuator open.</p></div>
<div><strong>Lever sticks or chair will not change height</strong><p>Investigate the visible lever/control linkage using the manufacturer's non-invasive procedure. A stuck lever does not prove the gas spring is empty.</p></div>
<div><strong>Under warranty</strong><p>Check the manufacturer or dealer process before attempting disassembly that could compromise parts or service eligibility.</p></div>
<div><strong>Replacement part unavailable</strong><p>Consider the repair cost, manufacturer support and chair condition rather than forcing a poorly matched cylinder into the base.</p></div>
</div>
<h2>What not to do to a pressurized cylinder</h2>
<p>Never drill, puncture, cut, heat, grind, or intentionally vent a sealed chair gas spring. Do not apply uncontrolled force to a jammed cylinder while a person is seated. If the manufacturer documents a special removal tool or service procedure, follow it; otherwise use a qualified service technician.</p>
<p>A proper replacement is the <em>specified compatible part for the exact chair</em>, not simply a web listing marked “Class 4” or “universal.” Document the model, part number and the manufacturer-recommended installation process before purchase.</p></section>

